          <rect x="140" y="18" width="120" height="52" class="f-panel s-hair" stroke-width="1.25"/>
          <text x="200" y="42" text-anchor="middle" class="ttl fg-t">PIPELINE</text>
          <text x="200" y="60" text-anchor="middle" class="sm mu-t">batch ticket</text>
          <rect x="272" y="18" width="120" height="52" class="f-panel s-hair" stroke-width="1.25"/>
          <text x="332" y="42" text-anchor="middle" class="ttl fg-t">INSPECTOR</text>
          <text x="332" y="60" text-anchor="middle" class="sm mu-t">quality report</text>
          <path d="M68 70 C68 100 160 100 172 122" fill="none" class="s-muted" stroke-width="1.5"/>
          <line x1="200" y1="70" x2="200" y2="122" class="s-muted" stroke-width="1.5"/>
          <path d="M332 70 C332 100 240 100 228 122" fill="none" class="s-muted" stroke-width="1.5"/>
          <rect x="130" y="124" width="140" height="48" class="f-panel s-red" stroke-width="2.5"/>
          <text x="200" y="154" text-anchor="middle" class="ttl fg-t">2 OF 3 AGREE?</text>
          <line x1="168" y1="172" x2="112" y2="222" class="s-data" stroke-width="2"/>
          <polygon points="113,212 106,227 121,222" class="f-data"/>
          <text x="124" y="200" text-anchor="end" class="sm mu-t">yes</text>
          <line x1="232" y1="172" x2="288" y2="222" class="s-muted" stroke-width="1.5"/>
          <polygon points="279,222 294,227 287,212" class="f-fg"/>
          <text x="276" y="200" class="sm mu-t">no</text>
          <rect x="16" y="230" width="176" height="56" class="f-panel s-data" stroke-width="2"/>
          <text x="104" y="255" text-anchor="middle" class="ttl fg-t">PAYMENT RELEASED</text>
          <text x="104" y="274" text-anchor="middle" class="sm mu-t">to the producer</text>
          <rect x="208" y="230" width="176" height="56" class="f-panel s-hair" stroke-width="1.5"/>
          <text x="296" y="255" text-anchor="middle" class="ttl fg-t">PAYMENT WAITS</text>
          <text x="296" y="274" text-anchor="middle" class="sm mu-t">a person reviews it</text>
        </svg>
        <figcaption>Figure 6 · Proof before payment.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <p>Canada has already tested the plumbing. In March 2026, Export Development Canada issued the country’s first tokenized bond, with regulators’ approval, on a private network.<sup><a href="#note-21">21</a></sup> The contracts stay in charge. Canadian banks still move the money. <strong>A person can always press pause.</strong></p>

      <h2><span class="eyebrow">Eight · The limits</span> What this isn’t.</h2>
      <dl>
        <dt>It isn’t a coin.</dt>
        <dd>Venezuela launched a token backed by its oil in 2018 and shut it down in January 2024.<sup><a href="#note-22">22</a></sup></dd>
        <dt>It isn’t an offer.</dt>
        <dd>Nobody can invest in Common Barrel today, and I’m not asking anyone to.</dd>
        <dt>It isn’t finished.</dt>
        <dd>Refining margins swing. Construction budgets blow up. The skeptics have a point, and this plan has to answer them in numbers before anyone pours concrete.</dd>
      </dl>

      <h2><span class="eyebrow">Nine · The precedent</span> Alberta and Quebec have done this before.</h2>
      <p>In 1962, Jean Lesage called an election on a single idea: buy out Quebec’s private power companies and fold them into Hydro-Québec. His slogan was <i lang="fr">maîtres chez nous</i>, masters in our own house. He won, and Hydro-Québec became a source of pride.<sup><a href="#note-23">23</a>, <a href="#note-24">24</a></sup></p>
      <p>In 1976, Peter Lougheed set up the Heritage Savings Trust Fund to put 30 per cent of Alberta’s resource revenue into savings.<sup><a href="#note-25">25</a></sup></p>
      <p class="view">Both provinces already understand the idea underneath Common Barrel. <strong>My view: when you own the asset, you stop asking for permission.</strong></p>
      <p class="pull">Each province alone is a vassal. Together we are a peer.</p>

      <h2><span class="eyebrow">Ten · The ask</span> Not money. Scrutiny.</h2>
      <p>If you build refineries, run a producer, sit on a pension investment committee, work in government, or help lead a Nation’s development corporation, tell me what breaks first.</p>
      <p>If you’re a Canadian who would want a share of a refinery this country owns, say so, loudly enough for Ottawa to hear it.</p>
      <figure class="mark">
        <BearDual />
      </figure>
      <p class="sign">Strong. Proud. Free.</p>
      <p>Jesse James</p>
      <p class="fig">Common Barrel is a proposal for public discussion. It is not an offer to sell, or a solicitation of an offer to buy, any security. A French edition is in preparation.</p>

